# Copy to `.env` (never commit it). All values are optional for local development.

# ── API ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PORT=8787
# Dev only: where the Vite dev server proxies /api to. Default http://localhost:8787.
# SLIDER_API_URL=http://localhost:8787
# Where PGlite (database) and uploaded files live; relative paths are from the repo root.
DATA_DIR=apps/api/.data
# ≥ 32 characters: signs sessions, encrypts Microsoft tokens and the settings of the setup page.
# Unset: generated once into ${DATA_DIR}/secret. To pin a value:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
SLIDER_SECRET=
# Encrypts files, comments, slide texts, deck titles and file names at rest (32 bytes, base64url).
# Unset: generated once into ${DATA_DIR}/data-key. Never change it – the API refuses to start with
# another key. Only set a new one before the first start; afterwards copy ${DATA_DIR}/data-key.
# SLIDER_DATA_KEY=
# Or a file holding the key (e.g. a Docker secret); relative paths are from the repo root.
# SLIDER_DATA_KEY_FILE=
# Public address of Slider (CORS, redirect URIs, links in mails); required in production.
# Formerly WEB_ORIGIN, which still works.
SLIDER_URL=http://localhost:5173
# Largest .pptx upload in bytes. Default 200 MB.
# MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=209715200
# Requests per minute and IP on /api/invites/* (accepting/looking up invitations). Default 30.
# INVITE_RATE_LIMIT=30

# ── Microsoft login for OneDrive/SharePoint links (BER-92) ──────────────────
# Optional: direct .pptx URLs and "Anyone with the link" SharePoint links work without it.
# Setup (once, see README "Link import"):
#   1. Entra admin center → App registrations → New registration "Slider",
#      accounts in any organizational directory and personal Microsoft accounts.
#   2. Authentication → Add a platform → Web, redirect URI ${SLIDER_URL}/api/auth/microsoft/callback
#      (locally http://localhost:5173/api/auth/microsoft/callback, through the Vite proxy).
#   3. Certificates & secrets → New client secret → MS_CLIENT_SECRET.
#   4. API permissions → Microsoft Graph, delegated: Files.Read.All, offline_access, User.Read,
#      openid, profile, email (sign-in to Slider).
# Restart the API after changing this file – it is only read at start-up.
# MS_* are also on the setup page; values set here win and appear read-only there.
MS_CLIENT_ID=
MS_CLIENT_SECRET=
# `common` (default) = work/school and personal accounts; or your tenant id to restrict sign-in.
# MS_TENANT=common

# ── Nextcloud (BER-135) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional: any Nextcloud works without setup – people type its address when they connect it
# (Login Flow v2, Konto & Anmeldung → Nextcloud). Set your organisation's Nextcloud here and
# nobody types an address; it may also live on an internal address or plain http (every other
# Nextcloud must be public https). Also on the setup page.
# NEXTCLOUD_URL=https://cloud.example.com
# "Weiter mit Nextcloud" through that Nextcloud: an OAuth 2.0 client registered there (Verwaltung →
# Sicherheit → OAuth-2.0-Clients, redirect ${SLIDER_URL}/api/auth/nextcloud/callback). Both or neither.
# NEXTCLOUD_CLIENT_ID=
# NEXTCLOUD_CLIENT_SECRET=
# "Weiter mit Nextcloud" for any other public Nextcloud (address typed, new accounts confirmed by
# mail, so it needs SMTP). Default true; false turns it off.
# NEXTCLOUD_LOGIN_ANY=true

# ── Login & organisations (BER-129, BER-130) ──────────────────────────────────
# People sign in with Microsoft (the MS_* app above – one login also grants OneDrive/SharePoint
# access) and/or a generic OpenID Connect provider; passkeys come on top once an account
# exists. None is needed to start: production without any login starts anyway and logs the setup
# page (${SLIDER_URL}/einrichtung) and a one-time setup token; type the token in there to set up
# the first login in the browser. Values set here win and appear read-only on that page.
# Signing in never makes anyone instance admin: sign in, open /einrichtung and enter the setup
# token from the log – that makes your account admin (the log shows it on every start until an
# admin exists, also when all logins come from this file). Without any login, development runs
# without login: every visitor acts as DEV_OWNER_EMAIL; claiming the instance with the token later
# takes over that account's decks.
# Force the dev login on/off in development (ignored → error in production):
# AUTH_DEV_LOGIN=false
# Who may create an account – the first login creates it: open (default; anyone who can sign in),
# invite (only invited people, no joining by domain), domains (verified addresses of
# SIGNUP_DOMAINS) or company (invited people and addresses at a domain an organisation is opened
# to). Invited people may always sign up, and so may whoever opened /einrichtung with the setup
# token while there is no admin yet. New accounts without an invitation get an organisation of
# their own, unless their company's is already there. Also on /einrichtung.
# SIGNUP=open
# SIGNUP_DOMAINS=firma.de,firma.com
# Delete accounts and decks nobody used for 18 months (on by default; also on /einrichtung).
# DELETE_UNUSED=false
# Days a login lasts without use (renewed on use).
# SESSION_TTL_DAYS=30
# The account the dev login acts as; whoever claims the instance takes over its decks.
# DEV_OWNER_NAME=Robert Hofmann
# DEV_OWNER_EMAIL=robert@example.org

# "Weiter mit Google". Setup (docs/self-hosting.md → Anmeldung): Google Cloud Console → Google Auth
# Platform, audience External, scopes openid email profile, client "Web application" with redirect
# URI ${SLIDER_URL}/api/auth/google/callback (locally http://localhost:5173/api/auth/google/callback).
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Google Drive as a source (BER-119) comes with the login: enable the Google Drive API and the
# Google Picker API in the same project. Slider asks only for drive.file (the files people pick).

# Generic OpenID Connect (Authentik, Keycloak, Zitadel, …). Register a confidential client
# with redirect URI ${SLIDER_URL}/api/auth/oidc/callback.
OIDC_ISSUER=
OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Button text on the login page.
# OIDC_LABEL=Weiter mit SSO
# OIDC_SCOPES=openid profile email

# Optional: invitations by e-mail. Without SMTP_URL the invite link is shown to pass on by hand;
# in development the mail is logged to the API console instead. Any SMTP account works, e.g.
# Brevo (EU, free): smtp://<login>:<smtp-key>@smtp-relay.brevo.com:587 – `@` in the login → `%40`.
# SMTP_URL=smtps://user:password@mail.example.com:465
# MAIL_FROM=Slider <slider@example.com>
# Where the website's contact form (/kontakt) is mailed; needs SMTP_URL and MAIL_FROM. Without
# it the form answers "gerade nicht verfügbar".
# CONTACT_EMAIL=kontakt@example.com

# Passkeys need no settings: their RP ID is the host name of SLIDER_URL – changing the domain
# invalidates every passkey.

# ── Plans (BER-130, BER-151) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A self-hosted instance has no limits. Only the hosted demo (SLIDER_URL on slider.bertro.dev) or
# SLIDER_DEMO=true puts organisations on the free plan: 5 people (members + pending e-mail
# invites), 3 decks (archived ones count), one own organisation per account, 2 GB of storage per
# organisation (every file of its reviews).
# SLIDER_DEMO=false
# Single limits anyway; 0 = unlimited.
# PLAN_FREE_MAX_MEMBERS=5
# PLAN_FREE_MAX_DECKS=3
# PLAN_FREE_MAX_STORAGE_BYTES=2147483648

# ── Paid plan via Stripe (BER-152) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Only on an instance with limits. All three together; webhook endpoint:
# <SLIDER_URL>/api/billing/webhook with checkout.session.completed,
# customer.subscription.updated, customer.subscription.deleted, invoice.payment_failed.
# Locally: `stripe listen --forward-to localhost:8787/api/billing/webhook` prints the secret.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_…
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_…
# STRIPE_PRICE_ID=price_…

# ── Automatic updates of linked decks (BER-107) ─────────────────────────────
# Decks imported from OneDrive/SharePoint or a .pptx URL are checked for changes (Graph cTag/eTag,
# HTTP ETag/Last-Modified) if someone opened or changed them in the last 7 days.
# Poll interval in ms; 0 turns polling off. Minimum 10000.
SYNC_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=60000
# Quiet time in ms after the last change before a new version is imported – PowerPoint Online
# autosaves constantly. Constant saving postpones the import by at most 10 × this value.
SYNC_DEBOUNCE_MS=60000

# ── Slide images (BER-94) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Slides are shown as PowerPoint draws them: linked OneDrive/SharePoint decks via Office's PDF
# (Microsoft Graph), uploads – and linked decks when Office is unavailable – via LibreOffice.
# Path of LibreOffice's `soffice`. Unset = `soffice` on the PATH, then
# /Applications/LibreOffice.app/Contents/MacOS/soffice. Not installed = built-in SVG preview.
# macOS: brew install --cask libreoffice
# LIBREOFFICE_PATH=/usr/bin/soffice
# How long one conversion may take before the deck keeps the SVG preview. Default 120000 (2 min), min 10000.
# LIBREOFFICE_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
# LibreOffices kept running to draw edited slides at once (about 0.1 s instead of 2 s a slide),
# each ~300 MB. Default 2, 0 = every drawing starts its own.
# LIBREOFFICE_WORKERS=2
# How long reading one PDF (and drawing its slide images) may take, plus 1 s per page; a PDF that
# takes longer fails its import. Default 120000 (2 min), min 10000.
# PDF_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
# How many decks are imported side by side, each with its own LibreOffice (~1 core, up to 1 GB RAM).
# Imports of one deck always run one after another. Default 1, e.g. 2 on 4 cores / 8 GB.
# IMPORT_WORKERS=1

# ── Voice and video comments (BER-116) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Folder for recordings; any folder works, e.g. a synced one (~ is expanded). Default ${DATA_DIR}/media.
# MEDIA_DIR=~/Dropbox/slider-media
# Storage now counts per organisation, every file included: PLAN_FREE_MAX_STORAGE_BYTES above
# (MEDIA_QUOTA_BYTES is still read as its older name).
# Per recording (recordings stop after 5 minutes). Default 100 MB.
# MAX_MEDIA_BYTES=104857600

# ── Stock photos (Unsplash) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# „Foto suchen“ in the editor and photos the AI picks. Register an app at
# https://unsplash.com/oauth/applications and put its Access Key here (the Secret Key is not
# needed). Demo apps get 50 requests an hour; apply for production with screenshots of the
# credits. The key stays on the server; previews load from images.unsplash.com.
UNSPLASH_ACCESS_KEY=

# ── AI ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each workspace's admins store their own API keys (Anthropic, OpenAI, Google Gemini or any
# OpenAI-compatible service) in the workspace settings – nothing to set here. Only for an
# OpenAI-compatible service on an internal address or plain http (a local Ollama or vLLM):
# allow it here. Off by default – it lets workspace admins make the server reach its own network.
# AI_ALLOW_INTERNAL_URLS=true

# ── Hosting (production; see docs/self-hosting.md) ──────────────────────────
# Postgres server, e.g. postgres://slider:password@localhost:5432/slider. Unset = embedded PGlite
# in ${DATA_DIR}/db. Migrations run automatically at start-up.
# DATABASE_URL=
# Built web app the API serves itself (SPA fallback to index.html). Defaults to apps/web/dist
# when NODE_ENV=production; unset in development (Vite serves the app).
# WEB_DIST_DIR=apps/web/dist
# 1 = behind a reverse proxy (Caddy): rate limits use the client address from X-Forwarded-For.
# Only set it when the API port is not reachable directly.
# TRUST_PROXY=0
